<article class="env-lemma" id="06WS">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06WS">Lemma <span data-tag="06WS">9.6.7.3</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $g: X \rightarrow Y$ be a morphism of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, so that the composition </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \Delta ^1 \times \Delta ^1 \xrightarrow {(i,j) \mapsto ij} \Delta ^1 \xrightarrow {g} \operatorname{\mathcal{C}} \]
</div>
<p> determines a commutative diagram $\sigma :$ </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ X \ar [d]^{ \operatorname{id}_{X} } \ar [r]^-{ \operatorname{id}_{X} } &amp;  X \ar [d]^{g} \\ X \ar [r]^-{g} &amp;  Y } \]
</div>
<p> which we regard as a morphism from $\operatorname{id}_{X}$ to $g$ in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$. Then a morphism $f: A \rightarrow B$ of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is left orthogonal to $g$ if and only if composition with $\sigma $ induces a homotopy equivalence of morphism spaces $\phi : \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, \operatorname{id}_{X} ) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, g)$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $_{f}\operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,B,X,Y)_{g}$ be the Kan complex described in Notation <a data-tag="06WE" href="/tag/06WE">9.6.6.16</a>. By virtue of Theorem <a data-tag="06WJ" href="/tag/06WJ">9.6.6.19</a>, $f$ is left orthogonal to $g$ if and only if the restriction map $\theta : _{f}\operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,B,X,Y)_{g} \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})}(f,g)$ is a homotopy equivalence. Unwinding the definitions, we see that $\theta $ factors as a composition 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  _{f}\operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,B,X,Y)_{g} \xrightarrow {\theta '} \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, \operatorname{id}_ X, g )_{\sigma } \xrightarrow {\theta ''} \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, g), \]
</div>
<p>
       where $\theta '$ is given by precomposition with the map of simplicial sets 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \Delta ^1 \times \Delta ^2 \rightarrow \Delta ^3 \quad \quad (i,j) \mapsto \begin{cases}  i &amp;  \text{if $j=0$} \\  2 &amp;  \text{if $j=1$} \\  i+2 &amp;  \text{if $j=2$.} \end{cases} \]
</div>
<p>
       By definition, the map $\phi $ is obtained by composing $\theta ''$ with a section of the restriction map $\rho : \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, \operatorname{id}_ X, g )_{\sigma } \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, \operatorname{id}_ X)$ (which is a trivial Kan fibration by virtue of Proposition <a data-tag="01PK" href="/tag/01PK">4.6.8.4</a>). Consequently, to prove Lemma <a data-tag="06WS" href="/tag/06WS">9.6.7.3</a>, it will suffice to show that $\theta '$ is a homotopy equivalence, or equivalently that the composition $\rho \circ \theta '$ is a homotopy equivalence. Since the constant map $\Delta ^1 \rightarrow \{ X\}  \hookrightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is right Kan extended from the vertex $\{ 1\}  \subseteq \Delta ^1$, evaluation at $1$ induces a trivial Kan fibration $\psi : \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, \operatorname{id}_ X) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( B, X )$ (Corollary <a data-tag="030M" href="/tag/030M">7.3.6.9</a>). We are therefore reduced to showing that the composite map 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  _{f}\operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,B,X,Y)_{g} \xrightarrow {\theta '} \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, \operatorname{id}_ X, g )_{\sigma } \xrightarrow {\rho } \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, \operatorname{id}_ X) \xrightarrow {\psi } \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(B, X ) \]
</div>
<p>
       is a homotopy equivalence, which follows immediately from Proposition <a data-tag="01PK" href="/tag/01PK">4.6.8.4</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
